# Ticket workspace

This directory is a ticket workspace managed by the `mgmt` CLI. Tickets are
Markdown files with YAML frontmatter. The tracker owns some fields; the local
files own others.

Cursor has no skill mechanism, so the routing that Claude Code gets from
`.claude/skills/` is inlined here. The behaviour is the same; keep the two in
step when either changes.

## Always start here

```sh
mgmt status
```

If it reports conflicts, say so before doing anything else — sync is blocked on
those tickets, so any plan that ignores them is wrong.

## Hard rules

1. **Never call the tracker's API directly.** No `curl`, no SDK, no scripts. The
   CLI is where every safeguard lives: schema validation, dry-run, duplicate
   protection, `local-only` stripping. Going around it discards all of them at
   once, and silently.
2. **Never edit `.sync/`.** Those are merge bases and cursors. A hand-edited base
   makes the next merge compute a difference that never happened.
3. **Never edit the `sync:` block** in a ticket's frontmatter.
4. **Never write to the external issue source.** The tool cannot; neither should
   you. It is someone else's repository.

## Field ownership

| Owned by the tracker — change it there, then pull | Owned locally — edit freely |
| --- | --- |
| `status`, `assignee`, `type`, `parent`, `priority`, `due` | `title`, `labels`, `estimate`, body |

Editing a tracker-owned field locally does not update the tracker. It produces a
conflict that someone then has to resolve by hand.

## Commands

Every command that could write is a dry run unless given `--apply`. Run it
without `--apply`, show the plan, and wait for agreement. `--apply` executes that
same plan, so the preview is accurate rather than indicative.

"What would a sync do?" is not permission to sync.

```sh
mgmt status                                  # counts, conflicts, cursors
mgmt sync                                    # plan
mgmt sync --apply                            # execute that plan
mgmt sync --only jira --limit 10             # narrow the blast radius
mgmt pull github --apply                     # mirror incoming issues (read-only)
mgmt promote issues/<file> --type Task --apply
mgmt new "Title" --type Task --apply
mgmt resolve <id> --take local|jira --apply
mgmt index                                   # regenerate INDEX.md
mgmt doctor                                  # token, server version, field ids
```

## Reading a plan

- `pull` — the tracker's value lands locally.
- `push` — the local value is sent to the tracker.
- `!` — a difference the tool will not act on unattended. Report it.
- `CONFLICT` — both sides changed the same field. Show both values and ask which
  wins. Never guess; the two values usually encode two people's intentions.

If the output says tickets were withheld by `--limit`, say so. A capped run is
not a completed run.

## Exit codes

`0` success · `2` conflicts (the rest succeeded) · `3` CLI/workspace version
mismatch · `1` failure. If an error names `JIRA_PAT`, the token is missing or
expired — say that without printing any value.

## Content that must not leave the workspace

```markdown
<!-- local-only -->
Internal notes, customer names, anything not for the shared tracker.
<!-- /local-only -->
```

Stripped before any push. If stripping cannot run, the push is refused rather
than sent — that is intended.

## Incoming issues

Files under `issues/` are read-only mirrors, overwritten on every pull. Editing
one is pointless. Promote it instead.

Titles are copied verbatim, including Japanese. If the project has a naming
convention, propose the adjusted title to the user and let them accept it — do
not rewrite it silently. A title that no longer matches what the customer wrote
makes the two systems impossible to reconcile by hand.
